{:mulog/duration 3631292, :mulog/namespace "ai.obney.grain.command-processor-v2.core", :mulog/outcome :ok, :mulog/parent-trace nil, :ai.obney.grain.command-processor-v2.core/command {:writes [:redacted-text-per-page :total-redactions :page-summaries :targets-applied :targets-missing], :command/name :sheet/set-repl-researcher-config, :command/timestamp #time/offset-date-time "2026-05-20T16:27:04.825219-05:00", :command/id #uuid "45be6c9d-a9b7-42ba-a17c-db27348b4dbc", :max-iterations 5, :mcp-tools [], :node-id #uuid "2e5f831f-72cf-4c77-ac08-c4081b4314ab", :sheet-id #uuid "452ebea9-6198-43d7-9077-20377c9fd19f", :instruction "Redact sensitive information from the provided document(s) based on the\n   redaction criteria.\n\n   For each page, identify ALL text matching the criteria. Every target you\n   identify must be a verbatim substring of the page text — the apply step\n   does literal substring replacement, so if a match fails it usually means\n   you transcribed the target slightly differently from how it appears in\n   the source.\n\n   Produce a structured result containing:\n     - the total count of redactions applied\n     - per-page summaries with redaction counts and the categories of\n       sensitive content found on that page\n     - a complete list of redaction targets with :page (0-indexed), :text\n       (exact substring as it appears in the source), :category (e.g.\n       'person_name', 'phone_number', 'address', 'email', 'government_id',\n       'financial_info', 'date', 'custom'), and :reason\n\n   STRUCTURAL VERIFICATION REQUIREMENT: For high-confidence answers, the\n   behavior tree you design and emit should include a verification stage\n   that surfaces any targets that could not be located in the page text\n   (after applying), and an adversarial second pass that re-examines each\n   page for any PII you may have missed. Single-pass identification on a\n   dense PII-rich document reliably misses some targets — the second pass\n   that re-reads the source with the first pass's targets in hand catches\n   them. Use behavior-tree primitives (sequence, llm, map-each, aggregate,\n   code) rather than coordinating multiple sub-calls inline as imperative\n   Clojure code: the tree is the durable, observable record of the work\n   that was done.\n\n## Available pre-built code-node functions\n\nPre-built deterministic functions available for :code nodes:\n\nFunction: ai.obney.orc.predict-rlm-redaction-tools.interface/apply-redactions\nReads: a per-page text vector AND a targets vector\nWrites: :redacted-text-per-page :total-redactions :page-summaries :targets-applied :targets-missing\nBehavior: pure substring redaction. For each target map {:page <0-indexed> :text <verbatim substring> :category <string> :reason <string>}, replace the target's :text in the appropriate page's text with a block of █ characters. Returns the per-page redacted text vector, the total count of redactions actually applied, per-page summaries with redaction count and category list, the subset of input targets applied, and the subset whose text could not be located in the indicated page.\n\nUse this fn for the apply step of redaction workflows rather than asking an LLM to splice strings; LLM string-splicing is hallucination-prone, this is exact.", :reads [:page-texts :page-images :criteria], :model "google/gemini-3-flash-preview", :rlm {:debug? true}}, :mulog/root-trace #mulog/flake "5A4YDH7M7sfNCfbcnBCCSh0xDqqedk5w", :mulog/timestamp 1779312424825, :metric/name "CommandProcessed", :mulog/trace-id #mulog/flake "5A4YDH7M7sfNCfbcnBCCSh0xDqqedk5w", :mulog/event-name :ai.obney.grain.command-processor-v2.core/process-command, :metric/resolution :high}
